From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 10:29:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] inotify : Don't lose the shutdown signal

inotify_handler_deinit() sends SIGUSR1 once and joins the thread. The
signal only ends a blocked read(), and closing a session removes every
watch, so the thread is often busy with the resulting events when it
lands. It then goes back to read() for good and the join never returns:
the process hangs on SIGTERM whenever a host is attached, until a service
manager kills it.

Send the signal until the thread has exited.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 src/inotify.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/inotify.c b/src/inotify.c
index 8f56637..5143068 100644
--- a/src/inotify.c
+++ b/src/inotify.c
@@ -23,8 +23,10 @@
  * @author Jean-François DEL NERO <Jean-Francois.DELNERO@viveris.fr>
  */
 
+#define _GNU_SOURCE
 #include "buildconf.h"
 
+#include <errno.h>
 #include <inttypes.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <pthread.h>
@@ -356,8 +358,14 @@ int inotify_handler_deinit( mtp_ctx * ctx )
 	{
 		if( ctx->inotify_fd != -1 )
 		{
-			pthread_kill( ctx->inotify_thread, SIGUSR1);
-			pthread_join( ctx->inotify_thread, &ret);
+			// A signal only ends a blocked read(). Closing a session removes every
+			// watch, so the thread may be busy with the resulting events when the
+			// signal lands, and it would then go back to read() for good.
+			do
+			{
+				pthread_kill( ctx->inotify_thread, SIGUSR1);
+				usleep(10 * 1000);
+			}while( pthread_tryjoin_np( ctx->inotify_thread, &ret ) == EBUSY );
 			close( ctx->inotify_fd );
 			ctx->inotify_fd = -1;
 		}
